Reply ServiceUnknown instead of dropping an undeliverable method call.

busd 0.5.0 answers a method call addressed to a well-known name that nobody
owns by discarding it: `send_msg` bails, `serve_peer` turns that into a `warn!`
and moves on. Nothing goes back to the caller. busd has no server-side reply
timeout the way the reference dbus-daemon does, and zbus imposes no client-side
one, so a BLOCKING caller waits forever.

That is not one application's problem, it is a whole class. A stock session on
x86_64 addresses eleven distinct unowned names in its first ten seconds --
com.system76.PowerDaemon, org.a11y.Bus, org.freedesktop.UPower,
org.freedesktop.locale1, org.freedesktop.timedate1,
com.system76.CosmicSettingsDaemon (six calls), and the APP_ID of each of the
four autostarted single-instance components. libcosmic's `run_single_instance`
is simply the caller that blocks on it, which is why no libcosmic application
had ever rendered.

The reply is gated three ways, all of them load-bearing:

  * only `Type::MethodCall` gets a reply. A `MethodReturn`/`Error` addressed to
    a name that has since gone away must be dropped silently, or the bus would
    answer its own errors; signals never reach here at all, since `serve_peer`
    routes them to `broadcast_msg` before this match.
  * `NO_REPLY_EXPECTED` suppresses it, per the D-Bus specification. Replying to
    a call that asked for no reply leaves an unmatched serial in the caller.
  * a message with no `sender` is dropped, since there is nowhere to address the
    error. `peer::Stream` sets the sender on every message it admits, so this is
    belt and braces.

`Message::error(&header, name)` copies the incoming serial into `reply_serial`
and the incoming sender into `destination` by itself; only the sender field has
to be set, and it must be the bus's own well-known name, `fdo::BUS_NAME`.

The caller is left better off, not merely unblocked: libcosmic treats the `Err`
as "no other instance" and falls through to `run::<App>()` with
`core.single_instance = true`, so the application then owns its APP_ID and
serves `DbusActivation` itself. Single-instance behaviour is preserved, which
is the difference between this and setting COSMIC_SINGLE_INSTANCE=false.

